  "decision": {
    "answer": "NEI",
    "explanation": "The provided evidence (pmid-27093365:f01) concerns Merkel-cell carcinoma, not cutaneous adenocystic carcinoma. The claim specifically targets cutaneous adenocystic carcinoma. Furthermore, the evidence explicitly states that PD-L1 expression did not correlate significantly with clinical response to pembrolizumab in the studied cohort, which contradicts the 'sensitivity/response' direction for CD274 (PD-L1) expression. However, since the disease entity is entirely different, the evidence is insufficient to support or refute the claim for the specified disease. Therefore, the stance is NEI (Not Enough Information) due to disease mismatch.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-27093365",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 442,
        "quote": "Eligible patients were at least 18 years old and had distant metastatic or recurrent locoregional Merkel-cell carcinoma . . . Samples were considered to be positive for PD-L1 if 1% or more of tumor cells expressed PD-L1. . . . In our analysis of tumors from 25 patients, neither PD-L1 expression on tumor cells (Fig. 4C) nor expression on infiltrating immune cells (not shown) correlated significantly with clinical response to pembrolizumab."
      }
    ],
